<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eifel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eifel is a low mountain range in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, extending into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Its volcanic landscape includes crater lakes known as maars. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period it was divided among many small territories, including the abbey of Prüm and the counties of Manderscheid and Blankenheim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eifel.jpg" alt="Eifel" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Walter Koch on de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eifelblick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Eifel is a middle mountain range in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, covering about 5,300 km2 (2,000 square miles). It lies between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> to the east, bounded in the east and south by the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>. It belongs to the Rhenish Massif, a rolling plateau formed by the erosion of the Variscan mountains, and its highest point is the volcanic cone of the Hohe Acht at 746.9 m (2,450 ft).</p><p>Volcanic activity began about 50 million years ago and continues into the geological present. It left cinder cones, basalt hills and volcanic lakes, among them the water-filled caldera of the Laacher See and the circular maars, of which the Pulvermaar is the largest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Buchenloch and Magdalena caves near Gerolstein show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> and modern humans lived in the Eifel in the Old Stone Age, and the Magdalena Cave shows visits even at the height of the last ice age. The Iron Age Hunsrück-Eifel culture takes its name from the region, and the first smelting hut north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> was built at Hillesheim in the 5th century BCE.</p><p>In Roman times the range between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> was called Arduenna silva, the high forest. The Eifel was an important economic region, with lead, zinc spar, iron, limestone and building stone mined, and the Roman road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> crossed it. The Eifel Aqueduct, one of the longest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, carried water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonia-claudia-ara-agrippinensium">Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium</a>, modern Cologne. The oldest record of the name Eifel dates only from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, when the Frankish Eifelgau covered the sources of the Erft, Urft, Kyll and Ahr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Part of the North Eifel has been the Eifel National Park since 2004, and four nature parks lie in the region. Much of it has limited infrastructure and few large industrial areas. Wine is grown along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and Ahr valleys, and the Nürburgring racing course lies in the Eifel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eifel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They are usually placed in the <b>Eifel</b>, between the Treveri and the Eburones.</blockquote><a href="/state/caeroesi">Caeroesi</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Manderscheid in the <b>Eifel</b> acquired Blankenheim and its castle by marriage in the 15th century and were raised to counts in 1457.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-manderscheid-blankenheim">County of Manderscheid-Blankenheim</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship of Arenberg, named after a castle in the <b>Eifel</b>, passed through heiresses to the Ligne family in the 16th century, which took the Arenberg name.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-arenberg">Duchy of Arenberg</a></li><li><blockquote>Later its abbot was a prince of the Empire ruling a small territory in the <b>Eifel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-prum">Imperial Abbey of Prüm</a></li></ul></section>
